my path in voiceover was very  non-traditional I actually have a degree  in business and a degree in Spanish and  parlayed that into working in the  Silicon Valley in California for ten  years working in all manner of startups  venture capital and and going to their  startup companies in building a company  from for people to hundreds and and on  and on and really struggling to enjoy  life so serendipitously I was I was out  with my husband and we were talking  about dream jobs and I said I'd always  wanted to be a voice on The Simpsons and  two weeks later I was getting ready for  work one of those fateful mornings and  Nancy Cartwright who is the voice of  Bart Simpson was on the radio to promote  the movie and started talking about  voice-over and how it was this  incredible job and we in the San  Francisco Bay Area had a school right  nearby that was incredible and I called  that day and I started taking classes  that week and two years later I had an  agent and I quit my job and moved to Los  Angeles and here we are today my first  job was as Katya in The Walking Dead  which was incredible you know it was a  round of auditions that came through my  agent in San Francisco I auditioned for  Clementine I ocean for Lily I auditioned  for Carly but there was something about  Katya that spoke to me but she required  a Belgian accent so I went out and I  researched Belgian as much as I could  and I had a Belgian friend and I  followed him around with a tape recorder  for two days and I'm sure he thought I  was a psychopath and and turned in my  understand for Katya and I booked it and  it was probably my second or third  audition ever as of as a as an actor and  and that was my first job and it changed  everything working with the team was  amazing it was so genuine and and you  know they really just wanted us to care  about this this person and it was my job  to make her live and breathe and  and to give her reasons to love her  hot-headed husband and reasons to hate  other things you know to be an actor but  because of that job I met some of my  closest friends to this day you know  Melissa Hutchison who's Clementine is  one of my best friends Owen Thomas who  is Omid in the series and Gavin Hammond  who's Kenny and you know we all met  after we had filmed after we'd recorded  this game and said oh my god you're  you're Clementine  I'm Katja can you believe they did this  to us oh it was crazy  but it was such a bonding experience  we're normally in games you could share  a hundred credits with someone and be  like oh yeah that was cool and it  doesn't really matter but this one was  just lightning in a bottle and and you  know also through that I met Shawn  Bannerman and jake rodman a rod Ken and  we went on to do a fire watch and you  know that game just changed everything  it changed everything  fire watch all came from the walking  dead  so I met Shawn and Jake as I mentioned  working on the walking dead  and Shawn reached out to me shortly  after season 1 had wrapped and he said  you know I've left telltale I'm writing  a new game with a female protagonist are  you in and as an actor that is the  greatest call to get and I said yeah of  course I said yes  and but it was you know a good two years  then until we actually kind of got down  to the Nitty Gritty of recording and of  course you know I went through the whole  range of emotion at that point if I god  have I been replaced have they found  someone else am I still in the running  is still gonna be me you know all the  self-doubt that we go through as actors  and they had me record a sample track  back when her name was Alice and and we  did you know kind of a test run for them  so that they could hear how I would be  as her and then they went through their  process of finding Henry which was for  them quite arduous they they auditioned  several people just didn't quite fit the  bill and and they couldn't find who they  wanted and then rich Sommer was talking  with one of the Camposanto guys on a  tabletop game Twitter feed and he was  like hey I'd like to audition and they  were like they're okay and he just  nailed it so from there we started  recording what they did which was so  wonderful as an actor was they let me be  in my home studio and rich was in his  home studio and Shawn would Skype us in  together and we would have actual  conversations actual dialogue which as  video game voice actors we typically  record and avoid and we might have a  director who knows what they're talking  about we might not but we never get to  interact with the other actors the other  characters in the scene and so to have  this game that really hinges upon  believable dialogue be a dialogue it was  game changing pun intended what I look  for in a character is something I can  really hold on to I loved  three-dimensional well-written  well-thought-out characters it's it's  really easy to write a flat character  damsel in distress or you know whatever  but I would I really gravitate towards  which I think shows in my resume are the  characters that are deeply flawed  somehow but they're never gonna tell you  how you know there's Joyce and life is  strange and and what happened to her to  get her to the point that she is at with  this man in her life and then you know  Delilah and and what did she go through  to get to where she's at and and Katya  even and I like finding the meat and  really digging into that which is not to  say that I won't do other characters cuz  we've all got bills to pay but I really  like finding well-rounded juicy  characters I think being able to pick  and choose projects is um it's a  double-edged sword if you're young and  you're hungry and you're just looking to  build a resume it's a it's harder to say  you want to pick and choose however I  think there are certain things we have a  social obligation to shy away from  people need to be cast racially  appropriately and if you can help it God  knows sometimes we get in the booth and  they're like by the way this character  is not white Oh  I think you know if there's if you have  a moral obligation to a company and you  don't want to do a commercial for them  don't do the commercial for them or do  the commercial and take the proceeds and  give it to charity I think it depends on  each person's station you know if  they're if they're in a place where  they're comfortable turning down work  great games in particular I think I  think there's enough work for everybody  my favorite thing about Delilah is that  the player has to decide how they feel  about her without knowing what she looks  like  they don't know if she's buxom they  don't know if she's beautiful they don't  know if she's x y&amp;z they just have to  decide what conversations they want to  have with her based on how funny they  think she is or how how interesting they  think she is and that's so rare it's so  rare that we're unclouded by physical  appearance in in real life and in games  I am hopeful for the future of roles for  women in voiceover and I in video games  and I think it's it's evolving you know  you're seeing more female leads and  options for female leads and even you  know LGBTQ leads and and non-traditional  leads non-white you know I think it's  we're getting into a place where making  games is becoming more accessible for  smaller teams and they're able to tell  different stories with different voices  and I think that's just going to  proliferate more and more and I'm here  for it I am here for it well I think  with games you have an opportunity to  make a proper game with fewer people you  can't make a movie or a television show  with fewer than I don't even know how  many people but you know fire watch was  made with a team of eight I know people  who are making games solo one person  writing this entire game I'm making a  game with a friend of mine you know what  I mean so it's it's not gonna be  uncharted 4  but you know it's gonna be its own  experience and we have that option now  whereas in games in in movies and  television it still requires a full crew  which is not necessarily the case in  games  I would say the difference between  triple-a and Indy is that I have more of  a say in my character which is not to  say the triple A's aren't open to it but  usually it's gone through such a process  to get to where it's at that it's pretty  written in stone  whereas Indies it's a little more on the  fly  you know there's option for discussion  about certain things and and an input  where we have less interpolates my  inspirations there's a lot of them you  know in video games voiceover in general  is such a supportive community just so  many incredible people who really  legitimately want each other all of us  to succeed and I have been so graced  with Melissa Hutchison from The Walking  Dead and Jennifer Hale and Courtney  Taylor and Ashly Burch and all of these  generous loving people who who are  willing to help their friends and and  and that's been invaluable you know  commercially I met Marilyn Wissner early  on and she she kind of gave me the  confidence boost that I needed and  really believed in me and that meant  more than anything I met my agent before  I was anything and and he kind of pulled  me aside and said you've got it  so work on it and find me when it is  ready for public consumption and again  that confidence boost was was kind of  exactly what I needed at the moment I  want all the roles I I would love to do  a female bond I would love to do you  know Wonder Woman I I want all the  things I want all the things all of them  best thing about voice-over is I can be  anything I have been an 80 year old  woman I have been a 12 year old boy I've  been every manner of creature in between  it doesn't matter what I look like it  doesn't matter my age it just matters if  I can do it believably  and interestingly that's it that's the  secret sauce the worst thing if there is  a worst thing about voice-over is you  know it's a gig economy I could have a  great year one year and a terrible year  the next tonight I'm always hustling for  that  for that next paycheck so it's trying to  figure out where that's going to come  from how to keep it sustainable how do I  keep doing it while I get older Oh God  just going down that path  yeah I'd say but you know I'm happy  doing what I'm doing and I I will be  bagging groceries at the grocery store  before I go back to corporate  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]

